  "rendered": "The aluminium-based entry with the highest Li-ion diffusivity in the lithium-diffusivity table is amorphous Li3.5Al2 O3 (am)[1], with a diffusivity of 7.1 ×10^− 10[2] cm^2/s at 300[3] K. Plain amorphous Al2O3 is far lower, at 5.94 ×10^− 17[4] cm^2/s at 300[5] K, so the lithiated amorphous aluminium oxide is many orders of magnitude more diffusive than amorphous Al2O3. The other aluminium-based entries also sit above amorphous Al2O3 except the crystalline AlF3: LiAlO2[6] at 1 ×10^− 11[7] cm^2/s, Li0.2Al2 O3[8] at 1.1 ×10^− 14[9] cm^2/s, and amorphous AlF3 at 9.26 ×10^− 16[10] cm^2/s, while crystalline AlF3 is lower at 6.2 ×10^− 18[11] cm^2/s. This matches the paper's point that coating oxides with different lithium stoichiometry show higher diffusivity than the parent oxides: the coating oxides with different lithium stoichiometry exhibit higher[12] lithium-ion diffusivity.",
